{
  "_help": {
    "itemGroups": "The headings in Recipes and in the admin's item lists, in this order (Luke, 26 Nov 2026). Each shows only once something is in it. An item goes under its own \"group\" if it has one, else: a tool under Tools, something to eat under Food, something you place under Building, anything else under Materials. A recipe goes under the heading of what it makes. Seeds go under Misc for now (Luke).",
    "items": "To add an item, copy one of the items above, give it a new id (lower case, no spaces) and set its name, plural, weight and look. It then shows up by itself in the Inventory, in Recipes (if a recipe makes it) and in the admin's Add list, with a picture drawn from its look. Shapes and their looks: rod (length, thickness, colour, twig, lashing: a twine colour tied round the middle), handle (length, thickness, colour, grip: twine wrapped round where you hold it, like the short tool handle; join: twine half way along where two sticks are tied, like the long tool handle; the tools draw their handles the same way and take grip and join too), flake (size, colour: a sharp stone), head (head: axe or pick, colour, lashing: a stone tool head on its own, with twine round its middle), rock (size, colour), ball (size, colour, leaf: a colour or false), axe (length, thickness, colour, head, lashing), log (length, radius, colour, end), knife (length, thickness, colour, blade, lashing), coil (size, colour), campfire (size, colour, stone, ash), spear (length, thickness, colour, head, lashing), fish (length, colour, belly), box (width, height, depth, colour), parts (a look made of pieces, so a new item needs no code: \"parts\": a list of pieces, each with form box, cylinder, cone or ball; size in metres (box [width, height, depth], cylinder [radius, length] or [bottom radius, length, top radius], cone [radius, height], ball radius); at [x, y, z] in metres from the middle, with x along the item's length and y up; turn [x, y, z] in degrees; colour (else the look's colour); sides for a cylinder or cone; also hold: palm, axe, rod or none, and grip for an axe hold: how far along x the hand goes). An unknown shape is drawn as a rock. stack (optional) is how many fit in one inventory square, if not settings.stackSize (tools and things you place are always 1). eat is what eating one gives (a berry gives hunger 10), which adds an Eat button in the inventory. tool: {\"uses\": 20} makes it a tool that wears out after that many uses (a wear bar in the inventory); a tool shows in your hand only while you use it (chopping, cutting), and only a tool on the tool bar (settings.toolSlots) gets used. startInWorld is how many lie around when the world starts (0 for things you can only make), and startNear is how many of those start close to where you appear. After changing items, run python3 tools/worlds.py test-build so the server knows about them. about (optional) is a short line shown when you tap it in the Inventory; without it, the about of the recipe that makes it is used. startInWorld: how many lie about the world; while there are fewer, one more turns up every settings.respawnMinutes, at a spot by spawnsNear (trees or boulders). A new item with startInWorld and spawnsNear starts turning up by itself, no reset needed (run tools/worlds.py test-build). group: which heading of itemGroups it goes under (only where the usual one is wrong: the tool heads are Parts, fish is Food, seeds are Misc).",
    "retired": "Items taken out of the game (Luke, 26 Nov 2026: twigs), and what any still in someone's inventory or on the ground turn into when the server starts: an item id, or null to just take them away. Remove the item and its recipes first, then add it here. Keep an entry for a while, so an old save or an earlier time restored is tidied too. Run tools/worlds.py test-build after changing it."
  },
